{"id":"storage-windows-storage","corpus":"k8s","title":"Windows Storage","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/storage/windows-storage/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Windows Storage"},{"kind":"p","text":"This page provides an storage overview specific to the Windows operating system."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Persistent storage"},{"kind":"p","text":"Windows has a layered filesystem driver to mount container layers and create a copy filesystem based on NTFS. All file paths in the container are resolved only within the context of that container."},{"kind":"li","text":"With Docker, volume mounts can only target a directory in the container, and not an individual file. This limitation does not apply to containerd."},{"kind":"li","text":"Volume mounts cannot project files or directories back to the host filesystem."},{"kind":"li","text":"Read-only filesystems are not supported because write access is always required for the Windows registry and SAM database. However, read-only volumes are supported."},{"kind":"li","text":"Volume user-masks and permissions are not available. Because the SAM is not shared between the host & container, there's no mapping between them. All permissions are resolved within the context of the container."},{"kind":"p","text":"As a result, the following storage functionality is not supported on Windows nodes:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Volume subpath mounts: only the entire volume can be mounted in a Windows container"},{"kind":"li","text":"Subpath volume mounting for Secrets"},{"kind":"li","text":"Host mount projection"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read-only root filesystem (mapped volumes still support readOnly)"},{"kind":"li","text":"Block device mapping"},{"kind":"li","text":"Memory as the storage medium (for example, emptyDir.medium set to Memory)"},{"kind":"li","text":"File system features like uid/gid; per-user Linux filesystem permissions"},{"kind":"li","text":"Setting secret permissions with DefaultMode (due to UID/GID dependency)"},{"kind":"li","text":"NFS based storage/volume support"},{"kind":"li","text":"Expanding the mounted volume (resizefs)"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes volumes enable complex applications, with data persistence and Pod volume sharing requirements, to be deployed on Kubernetes. Management of persistent volumes associated with a specific storage back-end or protocol includes actions such as provisioning/de-provisioning/resizing of volumes, attaching/detaching a volume to/from a Kubernetes node and mounting/dismounting a volume to/from individual containers in a pod that needs to persist data."},{"kind":"p","text":"Volume management components are shipped as Kubernetes volume plugin. The following broad classes of Kubernetes volume plugins are supported on Windows:"},{"kind":"li","text":"FlexVolume plugins"},{"kind":"li","text":"Please note that FlexVolumes have been deprecated as of 1.23"},{"kind":"li","text":"CSI Plugins"},{"kind":"h3","text":"In-tree volume plugins"},{"kind":"p","text":"The following in-tree plugins support persistent storage on Windows nodes:"},{"kind":"li","text":"azureFile"},{"kind":"li","text":"vsphereVolume"}],"lemmas":{"windows":["window","windows"],"storages":["storage","storages"],"pages":["page","pages"],"paging":["paging","page"],"provides":["provides","provide"],"provided":["provided","provide"],"providing":["providing","provide"],"overviews":["overview","overviews"],"specifics":["specific","specifics"],"operating":["operating","operate"],"operatings":["operatings","operating"],"operates":["operates","operate"],"operated":["operated","operate"],"systems":["system","systems"],"persistents":["persistents","persistent"],"layered":["layered","layer"],"layereds":["layereds","layered"],"layering":["layering","layer"],"drivers":["driver","drivers"],"layers":["layer","layers"],"creates":["creates","create"],"creat